---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Timeline } from '../islands/Timeline.js';
import { eras, palettes } from '@nostalgiaui/tokens';
---

<Base title="Overview">
  <h1>NostalgiaUI</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A design system that renders any interface in the style of a past design era, using colour
    palettes drawn from popular VS Code themes. Built on Base UI, shipped with an MCP server.
  </p>

  <Timeline client:load />

  <h2>Two independent axes</h2>
  <p>Every rendered component is the product of two choices.</p>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="tile">
      <h3>Era controls shape and feel</h3>
      <p class="meta">
        Border radius, border style, bevels, shadows, gradients, typography, spacing density and
        motion. An era never sets a colour.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="tile">
      <h3>Palette controls colour</h3>
      <p class="meta">
        Surfaces, text, accent and status colours, taken from a VS Code theme. A palette never sets
        a shape.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    Because the axes are independent, any palette works in any era, and the demo above changes a
    single attribute on one wrapper element when you scrub. Nothing re-renders differently; the
    browser resolves a different set of custom properties.
  </p>
  <p>
    Era effects that need extra colours, such as a 90s bevel highlight or a glossy highlight band,
    are derived in OKLCH from the palette's semantic colours rather than hand picked. That is what
    keeps {eras.length} eras and {palettes.length} palettes from becoming {eras.length *
      palettes.length} hand maintained themes.
  </p>

  <h2>Start here</h2>
  <pre><code>pnpm add @nostalgiaui/react @nostalgiaui/tokens</code></pre>
  <pre><code>{`import { Button, NostalgiaRoot } from '@nostalgiaui/react';
import '@nostalgiaui/tokens/tokens.css';
import '@nostalgiaui/react/styles.css';

<NostalgiaRoot era="frutiger-aero" palette="nord">
  <Button variant="solid">Click me</Button>
</NostalgiaRoot>`}</code></pre>
  <p class="meta">
    The packages are not on npm yet. Until they are, clone the repo and use the workspace.
  </p>
</Base>
